@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
<PageTitle>How to run planning poker online · Estiblazor</PageTitle>
<PageMetadata Path="/how-it-works" Title="How to run planning poker online · Estiblazor" Description="Learn how to run planning poker with Estiblazor: set up a voting deck, invite teammates, reveal private votes and save round history." />
<article class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">FROM FIRST CARD TO SHARED UNDERSTANDING</p><h1>How to run planning poker online</h1><p>Planning poker helps a team compare estimates before discussing the work. Estiblazor provides the room and cards; your team decides what the estimates mean.</p>
<h2>1. Create a room and choose your cards</h2><p><a href="/create">Create a planning poker room</a> with a descriptive name. Choose from @RoomTemplates.All.Length built-in templates, including Fibonacci, T-shirt sizes, confidence and risk, or customize your phases and text options. Each participant can select one card in each phase.</p>
<details class="surface"><summary>Explore all @RoomTemplates.All.Length room templates</summary><ul>@foreach (var template in RoomTemplates.All) { <li><strong>@template.Name</strong> — @template.Description</li> }</ul><p>Loading a template replaces the draft deck and keeps the room name. You can then edit, remove or reorder the copied phases and cards. Saved rooms and history keep their own cards when the built-in catalog changes.</p></details>
<h2>2. Save your management link and invite your team</h2><p>The creator owns the room. Save the private management link before leaving the creation screen. It restores owner access on another device. Share the separate invitation link or its QR code with teammates; they only need a display name. The QR code is generated locally and never contains a management secret. Never share the management link as an invitation.</p>
<h2>3. Estimate independently</h2><p>Discuss the work, then choose cards privately. Select a different card to change your vote, or select the same card to withdraw it. The team sees voting status, while vote values stay private until reveal. People with the same display name remain separate participants. Choose observer mode to watch without voting or counting as a missing voter. Owners can highlight the active discussion phase while all phases stay available for voting.</p>
<h2>4. Reveal and discuss differences</h2><p>The owner reveals the round, closing voting. Each phase shows its vote distribution and highlights differing cards. Observers are excluded from voter totals, and the revealed names and counts remain fixed. Compare the estimates and ask what assumptions explain the differences. Estiblazor does not choose a final estimate for you.</p>
<h2>5. Start the next round</h2><p>Starting a new round archives the revealed results and clears votes. You can add a title to the next round. Owner configuration changes apply to the next round. Discarding an open round with votes requires confirmation and creates no history.</p>
<h2>Return, reuse and share ownership</h2><p>Your browser's home page lists your owned and recently visited rooms. Duplicate a room to reuse its current deck with empty participants, votes and history. Owners can grant co-owner access or transfer ownership to a joined participant. A transfer removes every other owner and invalidates all old management links; the new owner is prompted to generate and save a fresh private link.</p>
<h2>Keyboard and accessibility</h2><p>Use Tab and Shift+Tab to move between controls, and Enter or Space to activate buttons. Card selection is announced and marked with a check. Use the arrow buttons in the editor to reorder items without dragging. Read our <a href="/accessibility">accessibility information and feedback options</a>.</p>
